{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795541165,"nonce":"1N/Z1mFbZDzQA28i164xnv3sGFA=","title":"Dragging a scrollbar thumb with the mouse does not keep scrolling","message":"Reported by the owner on 2026-11-24 in a window. Not reproducible headless, because Headless::advance paints a frame whatever happened.\n\nCause: the window does not repaint for DRAG events by itself (collect_mouse leaves DRAG out of the hover dirtiness, and the post-dispatch per-hit gate excludes DRAG and DRAG_END), so a drag handler that changes something must call stateful::request_redraw(); the gate's own comment names the scrollbar drag as one that does. Scroll::create_internal_handlers' on_drag moved the physics offset and never asked, so the thumb and the content moved only when something else drew. Every overflow scroll container uses these handlers (the Scroll widget, Div::overflow_*_scroll and CSS overflow).\n\nFound with it: a code editor's own mouse handlers treated a press on its scrollbar as a press in the text, so dragging the scrollbar also placed the caret and extended a selection (the caret ended on line 82 after a headless thumb drag).\n\nFix: on_drag requests a redraw when the offset changed; the editor records a press that hits the scrollbar (ScrollPhysics::hit_test_scrollbar) and leaves it and its drag to the scroll container.","files":null}]}